In article <4003@trantor.harris-atd.com> tcurrey@x102a.ess.harris.com (currey tom 76327) writes:
>
>Another possibility might be to have weapon specializations per character
>class.  I think that there should not be weapon restrictions, but 
>ability per weapon would be more realistic.
>
>For example:  A knight would be well trained in lancing and sword fighting.
>	      He should get some kind of weapon ability bonus.
>
>	      A wizard would be trained to use a dagger but would not 
>	      practice often with a weapon (sword).  He could still use a sword
>	      but not with the profficiency (sp?) of a Knight.
>	
>
>Maybe there should be a profficiency factor associated with every weapon.

I think this idea is a lot better than having a character become more
proficient at a weapon the more he uses it.  With this method the skill
level is fixed.  If let each character gain proficiency you also have to
keep track of what his proficiency is for every type of weapon.

Also by giving a wizard a -4 to hit if he uses a two-handed sword you
indirectly take into account the fact that wizards are usually weaker
than say, barbarians.  To balance this disadvantage for weaker
characters you could restrict the use of magic by other characters.  For
instance, there is a good chance a barbarian could not use scrolls, let
alone read spellbooks and memorize them.

Ah, you may say, but what about gain ability potions?  If my
Intellegence goes up shouldn't I be able to use scrolls and spellbooks?
If my Strength goes up shouldn't I be able to use a two-handes sword.
No!  Are you really going to learn how to read in a dungeon when
monster's are breathing down your back?  It doesn't matter how smart you
are, if you don't have the time to study (or as importantly someone
other than Fido to teach you) you won't learn.  Also if you don't spend
years practicing with a sword you won't know how to use it.  Does
someone know if you do more damage if you have a higher strength?  This
seems to make a more sense than to hit probabilities anyway.

As far as preists and swords are concerned, I think this should make
your god angry.  I doubt if a god would directly stop you from doing
something, but he certainly wouldn't help you later.  You scratch my
back I'll scratch your's.
